hong kong's usually bustling victoria  park behind barricades and police for  years it's been the scene of candlelit  vigils to remember those killed in the  1989 tiananmen square massacre  but this year it was blocked off shadows  of clouds on the empathy park  illustrating what activists say is an  increasingly dark chapter in the  territory's history  freedom is starting to decline the  freedom in hong kong including freedom  of expression freedom of action all of  them are weakened everyone is remaining  silent because they are terrified of  getting arrested  dozens of police were deployed to  streets around the park and the busy  retail district of causeway bay  i just came here and there are so many  police i found it a bit troublesome  police had warned the public not to  gather in groups to mark the 33rd  anniversary passes by were stopped or  questioned their bags checked even this  man who was carrying a toy tank he said  had bought at the shops before three  years ago we had the free  to memorize this mascara for the  tinderman square  but just three years  everything changed  this is very dangerous just brick and  some practice  this is the third year all public  commemorations of the event have been  banned covert was the explanation last  year and the year before  2019 tens of thousands gathered around  the statue of the goddess of democracy  she was built as a copy of this erected  in tiananmen square by pro-democracy  protesters in 1989.  that was months before a military  crackdown killed what rights groups say  may have been thousands of peaceful  demonstrators chinese officials say 300  died  the footage of a lone protester standing  in front of a line of tanks became one  of the most powerful symbols of the late  20th century  but since beijing implemented a  controversial security law in 2020 the  annual commemorations have become  smaller this year for the first time  none was planned  many protest leaders are in jail or in  exile and the goddess of democracy  statue has been taken away  i'm disappointed because although no  one's organized any commemorative event  the authorities are already on high  alert  but candles did burn in the windows of  the u.s consulate the white house  accusing authorities in hong kong and  china of trying to suppress memories of  the massacre those sentiments echoed in  taiwan china views as a breakaway  republic there a copy of another famous  hong kong statue was on show to  commemorate the victims of tiananmen a  sign that despite china's efforts their  descent will not be silenced alex  o'brien al jazeera
 

